```
=== DIAGNOSIS ===
is_anomaly: true
faulty_devices: spine_router_0_0
diagnosis_text:
The bgpd process on spine_router_0_0 was killed and has not been restarted.
At 12:21:29 the FRR log records zebra reporting "Client 'bgp' encountered an
error and is shutting down," an abrupt-disconnect message that appears when
bgpd exits without a clean NOTIFICATION — consistent with a SIGKILL or an
equivalent hard kill. bgpd is confirmed absent from /proc (ps aux shows no
bgpd PID while zebra, mgmtd, and staticd remain alive at their original PIDs
from 12:20). watchfrr, the FRR supervisor daemon that would normally detect a
bgpd crash and restart it within seconds, is also dead; frr.service is
therefore "inactive (dead)." Because watchfrr is gone there is no automatic
recovery path.

The mechanism for the simultaneous session drops is straightforward: when the
bgpd process exits, its TCP sockets to all three peers are torn down instantly
by the kernel. super_spine_router_0, leaf_router_0_0, and leaf_router_0_1 all
receive a TCP RST or EOF at the same moment, driving their sessions to Active.
Data-plane connectivity is preserved because spine_router_0_1 carries the
redundant paths to both leaves and to super_spine.

The on-disk /etc/frr/frr.conf is intact and contains a correct BGP 65100
configuration with the three expected neighbors; vtysh reports an empty running
configuration because bgpd is not there to serve it.

Root cause: the bgpd daemon (and watchfrr) on spine_router_0_0 were killed,
leaving the router with no BGP speaker and no supervisor to revive one.

Corrective fix: run `service frr start` (or `systemctl start frr`) on
spine_router_0_0. This will launch watchfrr, which will start bgpd from the
existing frr.conf. All three BGP sessions should re-establish within the
negotiated hold-timer period (typically ≤90 s).
=== END ===
```